The three sets in \Cref{eq:lifecycle} do not overlap: at each timestep after
its release, a task is either waiting, active, or completed. In this thesis,
a task becomes active when it is assigned to an agent, not when that agent
reaches the pickup vertex $s_j$.

Therefore, the travel from the agent's current position to $s_j$ is part of
the active phase. If the assigned agent is delayed by other agents during
this journey, that delay is counted as post-assignment blocking in $W_T$
(\Cref{eq:waiting}), rather than as queueing before assignment. This differs
from \citet{ma2017lifelong_mapf}, who keep a task in their task set until the
agent reaches its pickup location.

Let $t_j^{\mathrm{pickup}}$ be the first timestep at which the agent assigned
to task $\tau_j$ reaches its pickup vertex $s_j$. Then
\begin{equation}
  t_j^{\mathrm{assigned}}
  \leq
  t_j^{\mathrm{pickup}}
  \leq
  t_j^{\mathrm{finish}}.
\end{equation}
The pickup time does not change the task's state: the task remains active
from assignment until delivery. It only marks when the agent's target changes
from the pickup vertex $s_j$ to the delivery vertex $g_j$, as defined in
\Cref{eq:currenttarget}.

Only free agents can receive a new task. An agent is free when it is not
currently assigned to an active task:
\begin{equation}
  \text{agent } a_i \text{ is free at time } t
  \iff
  \nexists\, j \text{ such that }
  \tau_j \in \mathcal{B}_t
  \text{ and }
  \sigma(j)=i.
  \label{eq:free}
\end{equation}
Otherwise, the agent is occupied. For example, if task $\tau_1$ is assigned
to agent $a_2$, then $a_2$ is occupied from the assignment of $\tau_1$ until
the task is delivered. It becomes free again after delivery.

The service time of task $\tau_j$ is the time from its release until its
delivery:
\begin{equation}
  \zeta_j
  =
  t_j^{\mathrm{finish}}
  -
  t_j^{\mathrm{released}}.
  \label{eq:servicetime}
\end{equation}
This includes both the time spent waiting for assignment and the time spent
being handled by the assigned agent. Therefore, delaying assignment does not
reduce service time. For $\tau_1$, the service time is $\zeta_1=9$.